# AGENTS.local.md — local deploy notes (not tracked)

Copy this file to `AGENTS.local.md` (already covered by `.gitignore` — verify
it's actually listed there, this template ships no `.gitignore` of its own)
and fill it in with the real, machine-specific details of wherever *you*
deploy this project. `AGENTS.md` should stay generic and portable so it's
useful to anyone who forks or reuses this project on their own
infrastructure; this file is where the specifics of one particular
deployment actually live.

Typical contents:

## Deploy target

Host, IP/hostname, how you reach it (SSH key, Tailscale, etc.), OS/arch
constraints that shape the build (e.g. a low-RAM board that can't build its
own frontend).

## Co-located services

If this deploys alongside something else on shared hardware, name it and
note anything that must not be disrupted. (Example: a home-automation
system, a media server, another app on the same box — whatever it actually
is for you. There is nothing generic about this section; it only exists
because *your* target isn't a blank machine.)

## Hard rules specific to this deployment

Anything that would be wrong advice for someone deploying elsewhere but is
load-bearing for this one target — port choices made to avoid a collision,
"never build on-device", that kind of thing.

## Runbook

The actual build/transfer/run/verify commands, with real hostnames and
paths. `AGENTS.md` should describe the *shape* of the deploy process
generically; this is where the literal, copy-pasteable version lives.

## Scripted deploy configuration

`scripts/deploy.sh` (build → transfer → restart → verify, in one command)
reads its target from this exact section — required keys `DEPLOY_HOST` and
`DEPLOY_APP_DIR`, optional `DEPLOY_SSH_OPTS`. Add these three lines
directly under this heading, with no other `##` heading between this line
and them (the script's parser stops at the next `## `):

```
DEPLOY_HOST=pi.example
DEPLOY_APP_DIR=/home/user/workout-tracker
DEPLOY_SSH_OPTS='-o BatchMode=yes'
```

## Application environment

Injected into the container as env vars (`docker run -e ...`). Placeholders
only — the real values belong in your own untracked `AGENTS.local.md`.

```
APP_COOKIE_SECURE=0                            # 1 once the app is served over HTTPS; 0 for a plain-HTTP LAN/tailnet URL
APP_BASE_URL=http://your-host:8080             # what emailed invite/reset links point at
RESEND_API_KEY=re_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx         # https://resend.com/api-keys — never commit the real one
MAIL_FROM=Workout Tracker <you@your-domain>    # must be a verified sender in Resend
```

`APP_BASE_URL` is the single seam a future public hostname replaces — invite and
reset links are built from it, so getting it wrong mails people a link they
cannot open. `RESEND_API_KEY` and `MAIL_FROM` are needed only to actually send;
the app runs fine without them until someone triggers an invite or a reset, and
`scripts/bootstrap_owner.py` refuses to run rather than pretending to send.

`APP_COOKIE_SECURE` drives the `Secure` flag on the session cookie. Setting it
before TLS actually terminates in front of the app breaks login silently — the
browser accepts the `Set-Cookie` and then never sends it back.

## Gotchas and incident history

Deployment-specific lessons learned the hard way — hardware quirks, past
outages on this specific box, anything future-you or a future agent
shouldn't have to rediscover.
